Certainly! Below is a simple HTML and JavaScript example based on your mock-up. It makes use of basic HTML structure, CSS for styling, and JavaScript to handle interactions.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }
        .container {
            background-color: #e0e0e0;
            border: 2px solid #000;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 50px auto;
            border-radius: 10px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
            width: 300px;
        }
        h1 {
            color: #333;
        }
        [data-joke] {
            margin: 10px;
            padding: 10px;
            border-radius: 5px;
            cursor: pointer;
            color: #fff;
            background-color: #333;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>My Joke Website</h1>
        <div data-joke class="joke">I heard a joke<br>about programmers.<br>Why didn't they
            get far?</div>
        <div data-joke class="punchline">
            [push to reveal punchline]
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-joke]').forEach(joke => {
            joke.style.display = joke.textContent.includes('[push to reveal punchline]') ? 'none' : 'block';
        });

        document.querySelectorAll('[data-joke]').forEach(joke => {
            joke.addEventListener('click', () => {
                joke.style.display = joke.textContent.includes('[push to reveal punchline]') ? 'block' : 'none';
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

